    contentEn: `<h2>Getting from Zafiro Palace Alcudia to Palma Airport</h2>
<p>Zafiro Palace Alcudia sits directly on the beach in Port d'Alcúdia, on Mallorca's northeastern coast, as the first property in the Zafiro Palace Collection and one of the larger five-star resorts in the area, with 369 suites spread across its beachfront grounds. That beachfront setting is a major part of the resort's appeal, but it also places the hotel among the furthest from Palma de Mallorca Airport (PMI) of any address on this list: the distance is roughly 55 kilometres, and a private transfer covers it in about 50 minutes under normal traffic, a genuine cross-island run rather than a short local hop.</p>
<p>Port d'Alcúdia is one of Mallorca's most established beach resort towns, with a long stretch of fine sand, a marina, and a promenade lined with restaurants and shops that stays busy well into the evening throughout the summer season. Zafiro Palace Alcudia's scale as the first hotel in its collection reflects that setting, with a resort-style layout built around extensive pools, direct beach access, and the kind of full-service infrastructure that makes checkout and departure logistics for 369 suites a genuinely organised process rather than an afterthought. The resort has also been recognised with a nomination for Best Resort in the Condé Nast Traveler Readers' Choice Awards, one indication of the standard of service guests can expect around departure as much as during the stay itself.</p>
<p>Getting from Port d'Alcúdia to PMI means heading south along Mallorca's main road network, generally via the Ma-13 and then onto the motorway system that connects the island's northeastern coast to Palma. It's a well-travelled route with clear signage the whole way, but the distance itself means this transfer takes meaningfully longer than one from a hotel closer to the capital, and that difference is worth building into departure planning from the outset rather than treating the 50-minute estimate as a worst case.</p>
<p>As the first hotel to open under the Zafiro Palace Collection name, the resort set the template that later properties in the collection have followed, and that flagship status shows in the scale of the operation guests encounter on departure morning. A property built around 369 suites, extensive pool areas, and direct beach frontage naturally has more moving parts on a busy checkout day than a smaller boutique hotel would, so it helps to think of the transfer itself, a 50-minute drive under good conditions, as only one part of a departure sequence that also includes navigating a large resort's reception process before you ever reach the car.</p>
<h2>Planning Your Departure</h2>
<h3>Buffer Time and Flight Timing</h3>
<p>Because Zafiro Palace Alcudia is one of the more distant hotels from PMI on the island, buffer time deserves particular attention here. For a short-haul or domestic flight, plan to leave the resort at least two and a half to three hours before departure; for an international flight, an early-morning slot, or travel during the peak July and August season when both the main road south and the airport itself are considerably busier, three hours is the safer margin, and some guests with an especially early or late flight may prefer even more. The 50-minute drive time is a baseline for clear conditions, not a fixed guarantee, so treat it as the starting point for your planning rather than the full picture.</p>
<h3>The Road South from Alcúdia</h3>
<p>The route from Port d'Alcúdia toward Palma follows Mallorca's main north-south corridor, and traffic on this stretch varies considerably by season and time of day. Summer weekends and the peak holiday changeover days, typically Saturdays when many package holidays turn over, can bring noticeably heavier traffic as visitors arrive and depart across the island's resort towns, while off-season months and weekday mornings tend to move much more freely. A driver who regularly works this route factors in these patterns and plans the pickup time to avoid the worst of any seasonal congestion on the way into Palma.</p>
<h3>Checkout at a Large Beachfront Resort</h3>
<p>With 369 suites and a full range of resort facilities, checkout at Zafiro Palace Alcudia can occasionally take a little longer during busy periods, particularly on changeover Saturdays when a large share of guests are arriving and departing at once. Confirming your transfer pickup time the evening before, rather than deciding on the morning itself, gives you a fixed point to plan around, and means your driver already has your flight details and an estimated arrival time at the entrance before you've finished settling your account at reception.</p>
<h3>Pickup at the Resort Entrance</h3>
<p>Pickup for a private transfer happens at the resort's main entrance, the same point where guests arrive on check-in day. Given the size of the property, it's worth allowing a few minutes to move from a suite to the lobby with luggage, and resort staff are generally well practised at helping coordinate this kind of departure once you've confirmed your pickup time in advance.</p>
<h3>A Final Morning on the Beach</h3>
<p>Guests with a later flight often want to make the most of Port d'Alcúdia's beach and the resort's pools right up until the last possible hour rather than sitting in a packed room. A private transfer suits that kind of flexible plan well, since your driver works to your confirmed pickup time rather than a fixed shuttle schedule, and luggage can typically be checked with reception while you enjoy a final swim or walk along the promenade.</p>
<h3>Groups and Families Travelling Together</h3>
<p>Because a resort of this size attracts a substantial number of families and larger groups, particularly during school holidays, it's worth confirming vehicle numbers and luggage volume when you book if your party needs more than one car. For weddings, incentive trips, or multi-family holidays departing together, our <a href="/group-event-transfers/">group and event transfer</a> service can coordinate several vehicles arriving and leaving in sequence, so everyone reaches the airport on schedule without duplicated coordination effort on departure morning.</p>
<h3>Passing Through Central Mallorca</h3>
<p>Part of what makes the Port d'Alcúdia to PMI run longer than most is that the route passes through central Mallorca rather than staying along a single stretch of coast, skirting the market town of Inca and the agricultural plain that separates the island's north and south. This inland stretch is generally free-flowing outside peak hours, but it does mean the drive has more variety in road type than a purely coastal transfer, moving between the Ma-13 dual carriageway and the motorway approach into Palma. A driver who knows this corridor well can judge which sections are likely to be slower on a given day and adjust the route or pickup time as needed.</p>
<h3>Booking Your Transfer</h3>
<p>Arranging your transfer ahead of departure means your price is fixed before you travel, your vehicle is matched to your group and luggage, and your driver tracks your flight in case your schedule shifts. <a href="/get-a-quote/">Get a quote</a> for your departure date, check terminal information at <a href="https://www.aena.es">aena.es</a> before you set off, or browse our other <a href="/airport-transfers/">airport transfer routes</a> if you're planning further trips around the island.</p>`,
